+        "content": "A robo-advisor may not fit you if you want a person to call or your finances include accounts its questionnaire does not ask about. The SEC bulletin says some robo-advisers offer hybrid access to an investment professional and some use tax loss harvesting, so ask each one. A human adviser may not fit a small balance with one simple goal; see [is a financial advisor worth it](/guides/financial-advisor-worth-it/) for the break-even math. \n\nSpecific operational changes can flip this general advisory recommendation. If an automated robo-advisory platform offers a hybrid service tier with an investment professional at your balance, the robo-advisor may satisfy your need for personal communication. If you want to understand automated services in depth, read our overview of [what a robo-advisor is](/guides/what-is-a-robo-advisor/).\n\nConversely, if a human professional you interview is registered exclusively as a broker-dealer representative rather than an investment adviser, the regulatory baseline shifts. Checking Form CRS confirms whether your prospective advisor operates as an investment adviser or a broker. Reviewing your personal planning targets with our [retirement calculator](/retirement/) will help clarify which advisory model supports your goals."
